► **ANSWER**

## Analysis

The root cause is that the deduplication for `-u` happens **before** sorting. This only removes duplicates that are adjacent in the *input* order. But duplicates that aren't adjacent in the input won't be adjacent until *after* sorting. The correct approach is:

1. Sort all records (the comparator already includes an input-index tiebreaker for unique mode, so the first-in-input representative sorts to the front of each group).
2. Scan the sorted array and keep only the first record of each equal-key group.

## Changes

In `c_sort.c`, replace the block that does dedup-then-sort with sort-then-dedup:

**Remove** this block (the pre-sort dedup):
```c
    if (unique && nrec > 0) {
        size_t kept = 0;
        for (size_t i = 0; i < nrec; i++) {
            if (kept == 0 || !keys_equal(&recs[kept - 1], &recs[i])) {
                recs[kept++] = recs[i];
            }
        }
        nrec = kept;
    }
```

**Replace** the sort + emit section with:
```c
    /* Sort records (unsigned bytewise lexicographic). */
    if (nrec > 1)
        qsort(recs, nrec, sizeof(*recs), cmp_records);

    /* Deduplicate after sorting: equal records are now adjacent;
       keep only the first (lowest input index) of each group. */
    if (unique && nrec > 1) {
        size_t kept = 1;
        for (size_t i = 1; i < nrec; i++) {
            if (!keys_equal(&recs[kept - 1], &recs[i])) {
                recs[kept++] = recs[i];
            }
        }
        nrec = kept;
    }
```

The `cmp_records` function already provides the needed tiebreaker: when `unique` is set and the primary comparison is equal, it orders by `idx` ascending. This guarantees the first-in-input representative appears first within each equal-key group after sorting, so the post-sort linear scan correctly retains it.

## How this addresses each failure

| Failing test | Why it failed before | Why it passes now |
|---|---|---|
| `single-u-x-ties.p/.r` | Two `1 a` records not adjacent in input; pre-sort dedup missed them | After sort they're adjacent; post-sort dedup removes the second |
| `single-u-x-generic.p/.r` | Three `apple`s, two `date`s, two `fig`s, two `kiwis`, two `lemons` not all adjacent in input | After sort all byte-equal records cluster; post-sort dedup keeps one per group |
| `adv-manylines-u.p` | 100k records with many non-adjacent duplicates | Post-sort dedup catches all adjacent-after-sort duplicates |
| `adv-manylines-r-u.p` | Same issue with `-r` added; the idx tiebreaker in `cmp_records` is unaffected by reverse, so first-in-input is still first in the group | Post-sort dedup works identically; reverse only changes group ordering, not intra-group order |

